              {
                "text": "I'm trying to find the pattern behind which squares get expanded to 8 versus which stay at 2, checking positions across the training examples to see if there's a consistent rule based on location.\n\nIn train4, the squares that stay at 2 are all in the top half, while those that become 8 are in the lower half, which suggests the rule might relate to top-versus-bottom positioning rather than diagonal adjacency or column alignment.\n\nBut checking train3, this pattern breaks down: it has 2's starting at row5 and 8's starting at row0, contradicting a simple top/bottom split. Maybe the rule instead depends on the relative count of squares in each group, since train3 has four 8's versus three 2's.\n\nTrying another angle: perhaps whether squares touch or are adjacent to each other determines which stay 2 versus become 8. In train1, one isolated square stays 2 while two others turn 8, but the positions don't clearly support a touching rule either. I'm also considering a gravity-like idea where the lowest square in the grid keeps its color, but that doesn't fully fit either.\n\nNow checking column parity in train2 \u2014 the squares that stay 2 start at even columns (2, 8, 12), while the ones that become 8 seem to start at odd columns, so I'm testing whether column start parity is the actual rule.\n\nBut testing against train1 and train3 doesn't confirm this pattern - the column values don't line up consistently, and row+col sums don't work either. I need to find another relationship between the squares.\n\nI'm listing out the exact coordinates and colors of each square across all training examples, trying to spot whether position, adjacency, or grouping determines the pattern.\n\nNow I notice something promising: when I sort each train's cells by column index, the colors alternate perfectly between 8 and 2 \u2014 this holds for train1, train2, and train3. That alternation by sorted column seems like a strong candidate for the underlying rule.\n\nActually the rightmost column always ends in 8 across all trains, alternating leftward from there \u2014 so with an odd count of squares it starts with 8 on the left, and with an even count it starts with 2.\n\n",
